    "case_id": "231",
    "case_summary": "A 5-year-old Caucasian girl presented with an 18-hour history of generalized abdominal pain, fever (39 °C), vomiting, and constipation. Her past medical history was unremarkable. On examination, she was febrile, with a rigid abdomen and diffuse severe tenderness. Digital rectal examination revealed soft stool without blood. Chest examination was normal. Laboratory studies showed normal hemoglobin, white-cell and platelet counts, prothrombin time, INR, glucose, creatinine, urea, sodium, and potassium; C-reactive protein was elevated at 134.5 mg/L. An erect abdominal radiograph demonstrated air–fluid levels in the small bowel and dilated large-bowel loops. Abdominal ultrasonography revealed a 5-cm hyperechoic mass in the hypogastric region surrounded by intestinal loops. Non-contrast abdominal CT demonstrated a hyperdense midline mass above the bladder.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. urachal cyst\nB. Vitelline duct anomalies were considered given midline masses in children\nC. Appendicitis was entertained due to acute abdominal pain, but the imaging showed a hypogastric mass rather than right\nD. Granulomatous inflammations were possible causes of an acute abdomen, but pathology revealed acute suppurative\nE. Granulation tissue from the umbilical stump can mimic a midline mass, but histology showed an epithelium\nF. None\nG. None\nH. None\n",
